WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.640
Hello everybody.

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:03.040
My name is Markiplier, and welcome to Buried.

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.440
Now this going to be a different type of let's play

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:08.220
because this is an interactive story.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.980
And, it could be considered a game by many

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:13.760
and I consider it to be a game, but also...

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:15.460
It involves narration -

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:18.020
no physical actions to be taken...

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:21.040
and a lot of choices that determine your ending.

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:23.260
I don't even know how many endings are in this game

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:27.140
but it's a branching narrative... that covers five chapters.

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:30.120
And it could be something... really in depth.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:31.560
So let's begin.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.480
There's no time to wait around. We've got a lot of choices to make.

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:38.800
Also, the thing about these interactive stories is

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:42.360
while the visuals may be limited in terms of pictures and text,

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.640
the audio... is where it's really at.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.220
So I recommend you guys put on headphones,

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:48.560
turn up your audio...

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:53.280
And get ready... for a nice... story... with Markiplier.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:56.360
(Narrating) I open my eyes, and the first thing I want to do is scream.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.260
I'm flat on my back and everything seems to hurt.

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:01.160
The trees overhead look familiar.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.240
It's a clear night sky, almost beautiful...

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:07.620
Except for the fact that this means I've been out cold for at least six hours.

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:09.100
But there's something wrong...

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:12.200
I don't remember what, but something happened and...

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:15.460
My head... my god! My head hurts.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:20.380
And a ringing in my ears. Was there an accident? An explosion, I think.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:23.820
I remember Dennis screaming, but after that I can't remember...

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:27.520
As I sit up, I notice that my hard hat has been thrown off.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.980
I look around the area but it's not here.

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:32.920
It feels strange to go anywhere without my hard hat...

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:35.760
But I've gotta find the other members of the crew.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:37.080
They could be in danger.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:39.060
(Speaking normally) So I say...

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:42.000
You might waste time looking for the hard hat,

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.220
so it's best just to go for the crew.

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:48.760
You've already been through an explosion and you survived, so I know this guy's tough.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:50.700
I know that *I'm* tough.

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:54.220
(Narrating) I decide that my hat can wait until I figure out what's going on.

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:56.800
I get up and take a look around the logging site.

00:01:57.020 --> 00:02:00.500
I smell something burning... and don't hear any engines running.

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:02.260
Wait - something isn't right.

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:03.820
Was it the bulldozer?

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:07.600
Maybe the damn thing overheated for the last time; maybe the engine finally blew.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:10.940
What a mess... Well maybe I'm jumping to conclusions -

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:12.940
only one way to find out I guess.

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:16.940
I walk over to the center of the logging site... and dont like what I see.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:18.820
(Speaking normally) Dead bodies?

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:20.160
I'm guessing it's gonna be dead bodies

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:20.660
Oh!

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:22.300
Well that's not good.

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:23.440
Ooh - shit...

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:27.180
(Narrating) The logging site looks like a bomb was detonated right in the center.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:28.560
I dont know where to start.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:32.440
The one load we'd managed to stack on a truck for the day is overturned.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:35.300
The trailer bent, and the log's hanging off the back.

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:36.740
Where the hell is everyone?

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:39.500
My head is *killing* me and this *ringing* in my ears...

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:43.120
I can't hear anything. Not birds, not my footsteps - nothing...

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:46.400
It makes me wonder if I hit my head after my hard hat flew off.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:50.120
I run through a quick mental checklist to make sure my brain is still working.

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:54.880
My name is Roger Hastings. I'm 41 years old... The year is 2017.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:00.660
I own a small logging camp and we'd been logging in this strip of Kentucky woodland for almost a month now.

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:02.740
OK, so my brain works - that's a relief...

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:06.920
But it also makes me fearful... because something is certainly not right here.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:11.240
I look around the logging camp - my mind trying to figure out what has happened.

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:14.140
Just about every piece of equipment has been overturned or tilted.

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:16.440
(Speaking normally) Okay, so would it be more important to look at the debris...

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:17.740
Or look at the truck?

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:19.780
I mean, we can see the truck...

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:24.100
But I think looking at the debris would give a clue to actually what happened here.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:26.960
At least that's... *my* conclusion.

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:28.580
(Narrating) There are bits of debris everywhere.

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:31.720
There're fragments of the 5-gallon bucket I keep our work rags in...

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:35.300
A loose saw chain, and an overturned gas canister.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:38.620
Had there been an explosion, all the fuel on site would have started a fire,

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:41.480
but there's no charring, no burning - nothing.

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:44.020
I walk through the area where Dennis had been working.

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:46.900
From the looks of it, he'd been moving *slowly*...

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:49.240
He'd only taken down four trees today.

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:51.100
His metal lunch box was open -

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:54.520
an indicator that he had been taking an early break - but he's not here.

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:55.740
*No one* is here.

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:57.960
Where the *hell* is everyone?

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:01.900
I could try to make a call on my cell phone, but its battery is already running really low.

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:04.700
Not that it matters; the reception is crap out here.

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:06.420
(Speaking normally) So... it looks like...

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:10.500
Calling someone on the cell phone might actually be a *bad* idea - all things considered -

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:13.760
Because it *seems* - to *me* anyway - that that's obviously not going to work

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:17.080
because the reception is bad and I might save my phone battery for something else.

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:18.480
So I'm gonna yell out.

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:21.040
I'm gonna yell and see if anybody nearby.

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:23.820
HEY GUYS! WHERE IS EVERYONE?

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:26.680
TONY, DENNIS, FRANK, JOE!

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:28.640
But their names fall flat among the wreckage.

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:31.640
I get nothing back other than scaring away a few birds overhead.

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:34.880
Might as well start walking, try to find some answers.

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:38.800
The highway is almost a mile back through the woods, down the gravel road we used to reach the site.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:41.240
Maybe the crew ran that way for help.

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:45.180
But why would they have left me...? Were they scared? Out of sorts?

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:50.180
Maybe I can catch up to them. But with my truck turned over on its side, it looks like I'll be walking.

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:53.960
My god, I can't even remember what I was doing before waking up on my back.

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:56.320
Wait what's that underneath the bulldozer?

00:04:56.460 --> 00:04:58.300
Oh my god! It's a leg.

00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:00.320
It has to be either Tony or Dennis.

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:02.820
The dozer looks unstable, like it might roll some more.

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:06.600
Might not be safe to get close, but at the same time, I can't just leave him there.

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:08.480
(Speaking normally) Well...

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:13.040
All things considered they might be dead, but y'know what it's worth the risk to go look.

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:14.620
...I'm thinking.

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:18.500
(Narrating) "HEY!" I yell, "Are you OK? Are you alive?"

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:21.260
I skip over the stray logs and wrecked equipment to get closer.

00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:24.720
Seeing the dozer teeter makes me think... maybe I should've grabbed my hat.

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:28.060
I can see a bit more of the leg; the jeans are soaked in blood.

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:30.840
I recognize the work boots; it's clearly Tony.

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:34.820
His leg is bent at an impossible angle, nearly crushed flat.

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:37.900
The closer I get, the more apparent it becomes that Tony didn't survive.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:41.700
(Speaking normally) Best not to pull the body out! I mean, I get -

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:46.060
It's his friend, it's his co-worker... but he's obviously dead.

00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:51.140
There's no use in trying to... pull his body out, to try to get the -- the dozer will tip over.

00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:52.880
No, no need to do that.

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:56.280
There's nothing I can do for him now. I start to back away slowly.

00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:00.200
In my shock, I stumble back, suddenly tripping over one of the stray logs.

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:03.460
I bang my head hard on one of the logs as I fall.

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:06.560
I instantly feel a massive lump forming on the back of my head.

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:09.200
Damn... Why didn't I grab my hard hat?

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:12.220
But what hurts more is the realization that Tony is dead.

00:06:12.220 --> 00:06:17.280
Looking at him I feel something rising up in my throat. I don't know if it's a scream or vomit.

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:22.060
But I have to push it away... if I lose my cool right now, there are too many questions that will go unanswered.

00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:26.480
Shit - I can't believe this. Tony... he had two boys.

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:29.280
He coached his oldest son's baseball team.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:35.160
He was the hardest worker I had and was one of the most honest and reliable men I knew. This isn't right.

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:40.400
I can't stay here. I have to find Dennis, Frank, and Joe. I have to find out what happened here.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:43.420
(Speaking normally) So, I made a goof and didn't grab a hard hat, but I think...

00:06:43.420 --> 00:06:46.840
MAYBE... things might have been different if I did.

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:50.900
(Narrating) As I walked through the stacks of logs from the last week or so, everything feels frozen.

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:53.420
This high-pitched sound in my ears - it's terrible.

00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:57.260
Keeps happening every few seconds like it's coming from far away.

00:06:57.700 --> 00:07:02.000
I can't help but wonder, is it my ears... or is it something else?

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:07.580
The silence out here is creepy, and there's a smell... like the atmosphere after a bad summer storm.

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:12.680
I might as well admit it - I'm a little scared. Everyone is missing and it's dead quiet out here.

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:16.880
There doesn't even seem to be a breeze to rustle the leaves and branches.

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:22.100
My right knee hurts like hell. My head was hurting so bad before... that I never even noticed the pain in my knee.

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:26.500
I must have hurt it during the...well, during the what? Accident? Or...

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:31.040
Wait, is that Dennis...? I see him sitting on the ground, motionless, about thirty... feet away.

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:33.900
(Speakingnormally) Y'know what? Best not to -- best not to sneak up on him.

00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:36.280
I'm gonna yell; I'm gonna yell in case it's not Dennis.

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:40.440
Hey -- HEEEY! ARE YOU A MONSTER, DENNIS?

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:42.100
CAN YOU HEAR ME?

00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:44.820
(Narrating) Dennis lets out a shout, like someone waking from a nightmare.

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:46.700
He then looks back at me from the ground.

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:50.340
Yeah Roger, I can hear you... he hollers back as I head over to him.

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:54.280
Dennis is built like a wrestler... and has the tough personality to match.

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:57.360
But in this moment he looks disoriented and even a little anxious.

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:01.320
Though I'm glad to see him, the fear on his usually confident face is alarming.

00:08:01.340 --> 00:08:04.940
What the hell happened? What's going on? He asks.

00:08:04.940 --> 00:08:07.620
(Speaking normally) I'm not willing to bet it's an explosion.

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:10.400
I'll just say I don't know -- don't know - the crew is missing.

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:11.780
Tony's dead.

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:14.000
(Narrating) Missing? Where do you think they went?

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:18.580
I'm not sure, I say... but it has me pretty freaked out - something's not right here.

00:08:18.580 --> 00:08:20.880
Still sitting on the ground, he looks around the woods...

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:23.880
As if he's just now understanding the severity of the situation.

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:27.860
The equipment was overturned, the dozers too, I say - wiping my forehead.

00:08:27.980 --> 00:08:31.380
You okay? I ask him. Yeah... just shaken up.

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:32.260
Me too.

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:35.640
This might be the most intimate conversation Dennis and I ever had.

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:38.940
While he and I have always been on good terms, we've never been particularly close.

00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:40.380
I've always respected him though.

00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:43.540
Sure, he's come to work a few times looking like he'd been in a bar fight the night before...

00:08:43.540 --> 00:08:44.680
But I've also seen him do...

00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:50.140
an enthusiastic impression of a dinosaur as he played with Tony's kid while they waited for their dad to finish up his shift.

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:53.800
It's then that it hits me... Dennis and Tony were good friends.

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:56.600
I'm not sure I want to rattle him with the news of Tony's death right now.

00:08:56.600 --> 00:08:59.320
Not  before we know what's going on, but he has the right to know.

00:08:59.560 --> 00:09:00.400
(Speaking normally) Oh!

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:04.120
Yeah - you don't withhold that kind of information from someone.

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:05.780
You don't; you just don't.

00:09:05.780 --> 00:09:06.740
Tony's dead.

00:09:06.740 --> 00:09:08.960
(Narrating) You were upfront with Dennis about Tony.

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:11.580
Dennis's eyes fixate on me - startled.

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:14.240
He -- he was crushed under the dozer, I say.

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:17.060
Dennis pauses for a moment like he's trying to understand what I said.

00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:19.120
His eyes narrow and his bottom lip quivers.

00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:22.700
Shit, he says. And I can tell that he's fighting back tears.

00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:25.540
He and Tony had been tight - almost like brothers.

00:09:25.540 --> 00:09:28.340
Hey, he adds quickly as if trying to escape reality.

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:29.700
Did you see that light?

00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:30.940
No, what did you see?

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:32.220
I don't even know, Roger.

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:35.820
It was like this flare of white light; it came right up out of the ground -

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:37.360
Like an explosion.

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:39.740
So there *was* an explosion, I say.

00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:41.300
Maybe it was some equipment or --

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:45.400
This was *no* equipment... This huge ball of light came right out of the damn ground.

00:09:45.400 --> 00:09:48.000
(Speaking normally) What did it look like? It looked like a ball of light so...

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:49.780
Might as well ask where it went.

00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:51.460
He already said what it looked like.

00:09:51.460 --> 00:09:52.080
(Narrating) Where'd it go?

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:54.860
It just shot up into the sky until it was out of sight.

00:09:55.300 --> 00:09:56.500
When?

00:09:56.500 --> 00:10:01.080
I'm not sure... but I'd finished about ten trees for the day and was about to top one.

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:02.440
That's when it happened.

00:10:02.440 --> 00:10:04.760
You were almost done with ten trees? I ask.

00:10:04.760 --> 00:10:08.200
Yeah... y'know I wouldn't want to take a lunch break until I was done.

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:11.800
And that's when that light blew up. Why? You don't believe me?

00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:12.720
(Speaking normally) No, no.

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:16.080
I *believe* you Dennis. I believe you one hundred percent.

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:18.080
(Narrating) You *believe* Dennis's story

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:18.880
(Interjecting) Yeah!

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:21.480
(Narrating) He saw... what he saw... I think to myself.

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:22.920
He has no reason to lie about it.

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:24.740
My god - are you okay?

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:28.640
Yeah, I'm fine... But if I'm being honest, it scared me pretty bad.

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:30.780
And if it's all the same to you, I'd like to get the hell out of here.

00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:33.720
We both stand up and start heading towards the road.

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:36.520
It isn't until we start moving that I see Dennis is wounded.

00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:40.880
His right side is coated with blood - the red seeping through his blue work shirt.

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:46.000
There're splatters of it here and there... but also a very large red splotch... that makes me worry.

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:49.900
(Speaking normally) If I ask him if he's gonna be in a lot of pain, the big tough guy ain't gonna say he is.

00:10:49.900 --> 00:10:52.660
I'm just gonna ask how he got it. What happened?

00:10:52.660 --> 00:10:57.200
(Narrating) I'm not sure; I must have gotten thrown or hurt somewhat from that... light.

00:10:57.200 --> 00:11:01.060
It's then that I noticed the strange ringing noises still filling the air.

00:11:01.300 --> 00:11:04.340
Hey, do you hear that high pitched ringing noise every once in a while?

00:11:04.340 --> 00:11:07.440
Dennis looks *scared* when I ask him this question and he just nods.

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:09.780
I thought it was just me, he says.

00:11:09.780 --> 00:11:13.660
He looks out of sorts and uncertain. I've never seen him like this before.

00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:16.280
It's obvious that he's looking for some reassurance.

00:11:16.300 --> 00:11:19.760
Even though I'm his boss, there's something very unnerving about this.

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:22.540
We'll be OK. It's probably nothing.

00:11:22.540 --> 00:11:25.920
(Speaking normally) There's no reason to worry the guy. It's obvious he's shaken up.

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:29.200
There's no reason to make him paranoid about whatever the noise is.

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:36.660
I mean - it is what it is. We'll get to the bottom of it no matter what, but... he needs -- he needs some comfort after the bad news.

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:39.800
(Narrating) Let's be realistic, I tell myself. I might as well try to be empathetic.

00:11:39.800 --> 00:11:42.040
Out here in the middle of these woods, the ringing could be anything -

00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:44.680
Maybe a car alarm from through the woods or somethin'.

00:11:44.680 --> 00:11:47.400
Still, even my passive comment seems to have reassured Dennis.

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:49.840
He looks a bit calmer - more relaxed.

00:11:49.840 --> 00:11:52.800
We both start moving forward and don't say another word.

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:56.700
Did you try to call Frank? I know he had his cell on him today.

00:11:56.700 --> 00:11:59.060
Not yet. Luckily I still have some battery left.

00:11:59.060 --> 00:12:02.840
I pull out my cell and call Frank - happy I didn't make that call to Dennis earlier.

00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:04.960
It rings, but he doesn't pick up...

00:12:04.960 --> 00:12:09.980
But when I hear his phone go off behind the stack of logs, Dennis and I give each other a look.

00:12:10.340 --> 00:12:16.460
As the phone goes to voice mail, I hang up and we walk behind the logs... to find Frank's phone on the ground abandoned.

00:12:16.460 --> 00:12:20.040
We look around for Frank but find nothing - not a single trace.

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:26.300
It's then that I notice something really odd - a ten foot wide hole... in the ground right  near where the phone was.

00:12:26.300 --> 00:12:33.020
But there's no stray piles of removed dirt or rocks. It's clean - smoothly done - cutting into the earth... wide, but only a few feet deep.

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:37.440
It looks unnatural, like the ground was just deleted - no shovels or diggers used.

00:12:37.660 --> 00:12:38.960
Dennis walks over to it.

00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:42.080
This looks like the same kinda hole where I saw that light come from the ground.

00:12:42.080 --> 00:12:45.820
I say nothing. My mind is spinning but I don't know what to think.

00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:47.820
We need to get out of here, Dennis mutters.

00:12:47.820 --> 00:12:52.760
At this moment, that ringing noise carries through the forest again sending a shiver down my spine.

00:12:52.760 --> 00:12:56.560
Dennis is right; there's something strange going on, and we might not be safe here...

00:12:56.560 --> 00:12:59.900
But that ringing sound could be a clue to where the rest of the crew went.

00:12:59.900 --> 00:13:04.040
(Speaking normally) To be perfectly honest, we can't run away from the situation. We're already in the middle of it.

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:11.560
If you're in the middle of it, I -- I guess it would be the first inclination to go to safety, but Frank is still out there and he *could*... be alive.

00:13:11.780 --> 00:13:16.640
Tony is dead but... we gotta find Frank. And the only clue that we got is checking out the sound.

00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:18.740
(Narrating) You decide to check out the sound.

00:13:18.740 --> 00:13:21.860
Something's not right, and if there's a mechanical noise like that...

00:13:21.860 --> 00:13:23.720
You think someone is out there? he asks.

00:13:23.720 --> 00:13:28.100
Maybe. I think we should at least check it out. They might be able to help.

00:13:28.100 --> 00:13:32.700
Yeah, you go right ahead, he says with a grin. I'll be waiting for you here... Better hurry though.

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:36.280
Dennis doesn't look like he's in the best shape to be trouncing around the forest,

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:38.200
but we need some answers right away too.

00:13:39.020 --> 00:13:41.520
(Speaking normally) I didn't know that we'd be... splitting up.

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:44.480
Ughh, splitting up is like the worst thing to do...

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:46.800
But if I leave him here, is he gonna be okay?

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:50.400
Best to stick together, but he *is* wounded. Man - this is a bad choice.

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:55.800
I mean - if we were gonna get out of here, then he'd have to go anyway, so... Dennis has gotta come. I can't leave him behind.

00:13:55.800 --> 00:13:57.740
(Narrating) No, you're coming with me.

00:13:57.740 --> 00:14:02.000
I bend over and hoist him up against my shoulder. He resists just barely and then gives in.

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:06.180
He's looking at me like he's terrified of me now - making a moan in his throat.

00:14:06.460 --> 00:14:08.340
We start moving through the woods quickly.

00:14:08.340 --> 00:14:12.260
With no idea where the rest of the crew is, I worry they could be in danger.

00:14:12.760 --> 00:14:14.900
Every tree out here looks exactly the same.

00:14:14.900 --> 00:14:20.060
I grew up in the country and could find my way around the forest without much of a problem, but I'm feeling dizzy.

00:14:20.060 --> 00:14:24.060
I'm leaning against a tree when I see something that has no business being out here.

00:14:24.060 --> 00:14:28.620
Our logging crew has been out here for three weeks and I've *never* seen it before.

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:34.220
(Speaking normally) What is it? Somethin'... *dastardly*... in the darkness?

00:14:34.600 --> 00:14:35.320
Whoa.

00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:38.740
(Narrating) It's a small building - sorta like a mixture of a shack and a bunker.

00:14:38.740 --> 00:14:44.500
It looks to be made of concrete but moss, weeds, and plain old erosion has made it... look sort of mystical.

00:14:44.500 --> 00:14:48.160
It looks like something out of a demented version of a medieval fantasy movie.

00:14:48.160 --> 00:14:50.200
The hell is this? Dennis asks.

00:14:50.200 --> 00:14:52.760
(Speaking normally) I'm not gonna lie, I'm not gonna make a guess - I don't know.

00:14:53.140 --> 00:14:54.600
How would -- how'd I know?

00:14:54.600 --> 00:14:57.060
You purchased this land, right boss? Didn't you survey it?

00:14:57.060 --> 00:15:01.040
I certainly did...! I say getting a little defensive. But this wasn't here.

00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:04.600
Do you think the others... maybe went in here? (he asks nervously)

00:15:04.600 --> 00:15:06.420
Why would they do that?

00:15:06.420 --> 00:15:10.660
I don't know, but they aren't out here. If they aren't inside, they're probably dead...

00:15:10.660 --> 00:15:11.760
Like Tony.

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:13.760
He looks at the ground as he says this.

00:15:13.760 --> 00:15:17.580
I wanna go inside not just to find my crew but also to appease my curiosity.

00:15:17.580 --> 00:15:20.580
That ringing noise is coming from inside.

00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:24.040
But something about this place seems incredibly strange...

00:15:24.040 --> 00:15:28.000
Especially located all by itself out here - it simply doesn't belong.

00:15:28.120 --> 00:15:31.120
I wonder, do we knock or just go in?

00:15:31.120 --> 00:15:33.760
I can tell that he's trying to make a joke but it falls flat.

00:15:33.860 --> 00:15:37.320
I'm starting to think his humor is a defense mechanism for his fear.

00:15:37.460 --> 00:15:41.480
We both look ahead, take a moment to realize what we're about to do and start forward.

00:15:41.600 --> 00:15:44.660
We get no more than three feet closer when Dennis wobbles.

00:15:44.660 --> 00:15:46.320
(Speaking normally) Reach out to steady him.

00:15:46.320 --> 00:15:48.520
He's not gonna ask for help - big, tough guy like that.

00:15:48.520 --> 00:15:50.580
(Narrating) I reach out quickly, straining my injured knee.

00:15:50.580 --> 00:15:54.240
I barely make it in time, grabbing him under his arms and keeping him from hitting the ground.

00:15:54.240 --> 00:15:58.840
I'm okay, he said. Just -- can you help me walk for a little while? Just help me inside please.

00:15:58.960 --> 00:16:04.040
I can't deny him his request. I've never seen him this weak and it's a little uncomfortable to see.

00:16:04.460 --> 00:16:06.880
Makes me glad I decided to keep him with me.

00:16:06.880 --> 00:16:12.200
I help Dennis into the weird little building. The front door opens easily even though there's clearly a lock on it.

00:16:12.200 --> 00:16:15.840
Maybe the lock was supposed to keep someone in. It's hard to tell.

00:16:15.840 --> 00:16:19.380
I still hear that ringing noise. It's definitely coming from in there.

00:16:19.380 --> 00:16:22.940
Stretching out in front of me is a small interior to match the outside.

00:16:22.940 --> 00:16:28.140
The building is totally empty. There's a concrete floor and dingy... featureless walls.

00:16:28.140 --> 00:16:31.880
There are no windows... giving the place an even more isolated feel.

00:16:32.080 --> 00:16:37.480
Still, we hear the ringing noise coming from somewhere in here. It's *louder* now - more prominent.

00:16:37.480 --> 00:16:42.700
The interior seems confined at first, but a small set of concrete steps leads about five feet down...

00:16:42.700 --> 00:16:44.700
Revealing a corridor.

00:16:46.180 --> 00:16:49.160
(Speaking normally) I don't know about you guys but I am *enthralled* with this.

00:16:49.160 --> 00:16:53.120
And I'm already seeing - like - how my choices could have *drastically* changed the story...

00:16:53.120 --> 00:16:55.060
And there's a lotta choices.

00:16:55.060 --> 00:17:00.680
(Narrating) It's... a massive corridor in fact - like I'm in some kind of building underground. It seems to go on forever.

00:17:00.680 --> 00:17:04.560
This corridor is mostly dark with emergency lights every ten feet or so.

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:09.240
I assume we're now underground because this corridor extends far beyond the walls of the building we entered.

00:17:09.240 --> 00:17:12.120
I realize that someone might not appreciate finding us here.

00:17:12.120 --> 00:17:16.460
(Speaking normally) Well... we can't exactly walk quickly 'cause - I mean - Dennis is hurt.

00:17:16.460 --> 00:17:18.460
So, might as well go quiet.

00:17:18.460 --> 00:17:22.360
(Narrating) I feel like a ghost - like I truly have no business here.

00:17:22.360 --> 00:17:27.060
Dennis leans on me for support. We move slowly - our feet not making a sound.

00:17:27.060 --> 00:17:29.880
If anyone is in here, I want to make sure they don't hear us.

00:17:29.880 --> 00:17:32.500
We tiptoe forward as softly as we can.

00:17:32.500 --> 00:17:35.720
As I carry Dennis to the far end of this otherwise featureless place,

00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:38.200
I see a flight of concrete stairs lending down.

00:17:38.200 --> 00:17:41.240
I have to set Dennis down on the floor. His weight is killing me.

00:17:41.340 --> 00:17:43.120
Dennis, are you okay?

00:17:43.120 --> 00:17:46.280
Stop worrying man. I'll be okay; I just need a minute to rest.

00:17:46.280 --> 00:17:48.380
But looking at him I'm not so sure.

00:17:48.380 --> 00:17:51.640
I can still hear the ringing noise every so often - coming from somewhere in here.

00:17:51.640 --> 00:17:55.960
I look ahead at the concrete stairs pointing towards them, so that Dennis sees them.

00:17:55.960 --> 00:17:58.140
Can you make it down those stairs? I ask.

00:17:58.140 --> 00:18:02.140
He stands up slowly and is visibly making an effort to not fall back down again.

00:18:02.140 --> 00:18:04.580
Yeah, he says... sounds a little irritated.

00:18:04.580 --> 00:18:07.800
He starts for the stairs without me and I have no choice but to follow.

00:18:07.800 --> 00:18:10.060
We approach the stairs and by just looking at them...

00:18:10.060 --> 00:18:12.940
I get the sense that we're crossing some sort of line here.

00:18:12.940 --> 00:18:14.940
There may very well be no going back.

00:18:15.560 --> 00:18:18.220
(Speaking normally) Well! Might as well keep moving forward then.

00:18:18.440 --> 00:18:20.140
Not much of a choice to be --

00:18:20.140 --> 00:18:21.900
Ooh... that reminds me of SCP.

00:18:21.900 --> 00:18:24.580
What was it? The stair one - 087B?

00:18:24.580 --> 00:18:27.880
(Narrating) As I head down the stairs, I notice that they look aged.

00:18:27.880 --> 00:18:31.180
The damn ringing noises somewhere below... louder than ever.

00:18:31.180 --> 00:18:34.240
I feel like I'm walking down into the cellar of a haunted house -

00:18:34.240 --> 00:18:37.880
The light becoming scarce as the stairs take a slight turn.

00:18:37.880 --> 00:18:39.780
I can feel the temperature drop.

00:18:39.780 --> 00:18:42.640
Dennis makes it just a few steps down before he's leaning on the wall.

00:18:42.640 --> 00:18:45.240
He's breathing like he just finished a marathon.

00:18:45.240 --> 00:18:49.140
(Speaking normally) I gotta give him a break. He's pushing himself. He's probably losing blood.

00:18:49.140 --> 00:18:50.980
I gotta take a break.

00:18:50.980 --> 00:18:54.060
Let's take a break I say - putting my hand towards his shoulder to help steady him.

00:18:54.060 --> 00:18:55.240
Dennis lets out a sigh.

00:18:55.240 --> 00:18:57.680
Thanks. I don't feel too great, boss.

00:18:57.680 --> 00:19:02.580
As we both squat down on a stair, my mind seems to lock in on the unknown fate of the rest of my crew.

00:19:02.580 --> 00:19:07.860
Frank is married and I met his wife a few times - a nice woman with a huge laugh.

00:19:07.860 --> 00:19:13.460
My heart sags a bit when I realize I might never get to hear him belting out a classic... rock tunes during lunch break.

00:19:13.460 --> 00:19:14.740
Joe.

00:19:14.740 --> 00:19:19.000
Well, I don't know too much about him. He's twenty two years old and is considering community college.

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:22.900
His folks are deadbeats so he'd been providing for himself since the age of sixteen.

00:19:22.900 --> 00:19:29.280
Maybe I can still find them... and still get to hear Frank singing in his deep baritone voice after a few too many drinks.

00:19:29.280 --> 00:19:32.820
After a few moments, Dennis says: Alright, let's keep goin'.

00:19:32.820 --> 00:19:34.820
We both stand up - leaning on each other.

00:19:34.820 --> 00:19:38.860
I have to make Dennis stay behind me as I stick my foot out in search of the next step.

00:19:38.860 --> 00:19:42.960
I can't help feeling like a child as a very powerful fear seizes me.

00:19:42.960 --> 00:19:46.080
I'm expecting a monster to reach out of the darkness...

00:19:46.080 --> 00:19:47.440
Slicing into my throat.

00:19:47.440 --> 00:19:50.820
After what feels like forever, we come to the bottom of the stairs.

00:19:50.820 --> 00:19:53.200
We're closer to that ringing noise now.

00:19:53.200 --> 00:19:57.940
A door sits securely in the wall and looks just as out of place down here as I feel.

00:19:57.940 --> 00:20:01.380
A sliver of light seeps through - illuminating the area.

00:20:01.380 --> 00:20:04.520
The door has what looks like a panel with a lot of labels and lights.

00:20:04.520 --> 00:20:10.100
Small embossed print above the panel says... level one... entrance gate. Transport sector.

00:20:10.100 --> 00:20:12.100
And there's a button with no label.

00:20:12.100 --> 00:20:15.220
(Speaking strangely) *Bes~vehh* -- best not to be pushing random buttons!

00:20:15.220 --> 00:20:17.220
Let's inspect the panel first.

00:20:17.220 --> 00:20:20.560
(Narrating) The panel looks like some kind of diagnostic or alert system of some kind -

00:20:20.560 --> 00:20:23.040
Each label meant to communicate some status.

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:26.480
Lockdown engaged, experiment in progress, shutdown engaged...

00:20:26.480 --> 00:20:30.900
All these appear to be off, but one of the phrases has a slowly blinking red light next to it -

00:20:30.900 --> 00:20:33.100
The only powered light on this whole thing.

00:20:33.100 --> 00:20:35.800
*Abnormal* entity breach.

00:20:35.800 --> 00:20:37.620
What the hell?

00:20:37.620 --> 00:20:41.480
I slowly push the door open then Dennis suddenly steps in front of me - taking the lead.

00:20:41.480 --> 00:20:42.780
Let's go, he says.

00:20:42.780 --> 00:20:45.480
I wanna find out what the hell this place is.

00:20:45.480 --> 00:20:49.440
As Dennis walks through, that ringing or beeping or whatever it was gets a little louder.

00:20:49.780 --> 00:20:52.220
The door closes behind us with a click.

00:20:52.220 --> 00:20:55.700
I can't help but notice the wobble in Dennis's step as he lets out a grunt.

00:20:55.700 --> 00:20:58.140
You sure you're healthy enough to keep going? I ask.

00:20:58.140 --> 00:21:00.760
It doesn't matter. I don't want to be in the woods right now.

00:21:00.760 --> 00:21:03.580
He seems scared - something I've never seen before.

00:21:03.580 --> 00:21:07.280
Angry - yes. Upset - yes. But Dennis is never... *scared*.

00:21:07.280 --> 00:21:08.540
What did you see?

00:21:08.540 --> 00:21:10.640
(Speaking normally) What did he see in those woods?

00:21:10.640 --> 00:21:12.740
(Narrating) I don't know what it was, boss. It was like something --

00:21:12.740 --> 00:21:15.140
Someone'd left a radio on - crackling.

00:21:15.140 --> 00:21:17.140
But we don't have any radios at the site.

00:21:17.140 --> 00:21:18.680
It was -- I dunno...!

00:21:18.680 --> 00:21:20.840
It was like something in the forest was *moving*.

00:21:20.840 --> 00:21:24.140
I gave him a doubtful look and he cast his eyes to the ground - frustrated.

00:21:24.140 --> 00:21:27.240
I know how it sounds, but I know what I felt.

00:21:27.240 --> 00:21:29.460
It was like walking into a funeral parlor -

00:21:29.460 --> 00:21:31.700
Feeling *death* and gloom everywhere.

00:21:31.700 --> 00:21:36.060
I've never been so preoccupied with my own fear that I nearly forgot about the ringing noise.

00:21:36.060 --> 00:21:38.160
It'd just become background noise.

00:21:38.160 --> 00:21:40.940
We took a few more steps and I can now see where we are.

00:21:41.080 --> 00:21:42.600
It makes no sense.

00:21:42.600 --> 00:21:44.880
Yet another thing that simply seems out of place.

00:21:44.880 --> 00:21:49.100
The shapes and muted colors of metal are easy to identify, but it just doesn't belong here.

00:21:49.340 --> 00:21:52.080
END! - of chapter one.

00:21:52.080 --> 00:21:54.520
(Speaking normally) Well. That is the end of chapter one...

00:21:55.440 --> 00:21:57.340
And... that's fascinating.

00:21:57.680 --> 00:22:02.780
I -- ih -- I -- I didn't expect it to be something like this, but the story... is really interesting.

00:22:02.780 --> 00:22:03.940
(Reading) Respect the dead.

00:22:03.940 --> 00:22:07.360
You and thirty-six... percent of players left Tony's body where you found it.

00:22:07.360 --> 00:22:11.720
You and seventy-six... percent of players were upfront about Dennis about Tony's death.

00:22:11.720 --> 00:22:15.520
You and fifty-five percent told Dennis you believed his story.

00:22:15.520 --> 00:22:19.280
You and seventy-five percent of players went to investigate the ringing sound.

00:22:19.280 --> 00:22:23.400
You and sixty-four percent of players decided to bring Dennis... *with* you.

00:22:23.400 --> 00:22:24.860
(Not reading) That's fascinating.

00:22:24.860 --> 00:22:26.700
So, anyway - I'm gonna end this one here.

00:22:26.700 --> 00:22:29.420
Lemme know what you guys think of this, 'cause this is a different type of game...

00:22:29.420 --> 00:22:32.400
but I'm already fascinated about what's going on here.

00:22:32.400 --> 00:22:37.120
I really kinda wanna continue this, and I could make this an easy five part series.

00:22:37.120 --> 00:22:41.000
It'd just be... really interesting to see what it leads to, because...

00:22:41.000 --> 00:22:43.000
If I'm not mistaken here,

00:22:43.000 --> 00:22:48.000
this almost seems like an SC -- Containment Breach type thing.

00:22:48.000 --> 00:22:51.320
It said "abnormal entity breach"...

00:22:51.320 --> 00:22:53.880
Or escape... or whatever that was...

00:22:54.140 --> 00:23:02.000
And it's some *weird* facility that's *underground* with some *strange* mechanical *containment* type features...

00:23:02.000 --> 00:23:07.180
It may not be exactly related but... you can't help but see some of the similarities.

00:23:07.180 --> 00:23:11.360
And anything with SCP Containment Breach... is a fascinating experience.

00:23:11.360 --> 00:23:12.400
So, once again...!

00:23:12.400 --> 00:23:16.140
Let me know what you thought of this down in the comments below - I really do wanna know.

00:23:16.360 --> 00:23:19.260
And I really do wanna know if you guys want to see more.

00:23:19.260 --> 00:23:21.460
So, *thank you* everybody so much for watching!

00:23:21.460 --> 00:23:23.620
And as always, I will see you...

00:23:23.740 --> 00:23:25.460
In the next video!

00:23:25.460 --> 00:23:26.760
BUH-BYE!

